I'm well acquainted with the concept, although what I am proposing would 
be better described as "obfuscation".

Please remember we are not trying to secure a corporate intranet or 
financial transactions. We are attempting to stop people cheating at a 
computer game for one month out of twelve. If we can slow down hackers 
such that it takes more than a month to hack, we've won. If we can make 
hacking less fun than playing, we've won.

Even a small amount of obfuscation would IMO be helpful. A FLOSS Python 
client would be trivial to hack. A bit of obfuscation would not stop 
someone determined to cheat, but it may stop someone not determined but 
who may otherwise have succumbed to momentary temptation.

In any case, there's little point in debating in depth a feature of a 
project that may never to be completed. Or started.


